  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 255 PM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nSunday - Thursday Night...\n\nAn upper level ridge will begin to build into the region from the \nwest, bringing higher surface pressure. This will bring drier and \nwarmer temperatures into the forecast. Maximum temperatures for \nSunday and Monday will be in the mid to upper 80s, however Tuesday \nwill warm into the low 90s. LREF guidance shows these temperatures \nin the 90th percentile of climatology for this time of year. \n\nPoPs are expected to stay minimal for the beginning of the week, with \nslight chances of scattered showers on Wednesday. As the upper ridge \ncontinues east, the flow aloft will shift more southwesterly \nbringing some moist back into the region. Confidence is fairly low \nwith any developing rain due to the effect on the ridge from the \nupper level trough over Canada. A draping cold front will try to \npush into the Ohio Valley mid week, however some guidance is \nsuggesting it will become nearly stationary near the Great Lakes. \nThis will decrease the chances for rain as this would be the lifting \nmechanism for development. Otherwise, temperatures will remain near \nnormal with highs in the high to mid 80s through late next week.",
